 "/blog": "<h1>Carnosine Science, Explained.</h1>\n<p>No hype. No marketing spin. Just the science behind carnosine, muscle performance, and why the evidence matters.</p>\n<h2>Carnosine in Endurance Sports: Does Muscle Buffering Matter Beyond the Sprint?</h2>\n<p>Endurance events are mostly aerobic, but the surges, climbs and finishing kicks are not. Here is where carnosine&#x27;s buffering role may be relevant.</p>\n<p>9 November 2026</p>\n<h2>Carnosine and High-Altitude Performance: Does Muscle Buffering Matter When Oxygen Is Scarce?</h2>\n<p>Altitude raises the relative intensity of every effort. Here is what is known, plausible and untested about muscle buffering and carnosine up high.</p>\n<p>2 November 2026</p>\n<h2>Trained vs Untrained: How Muscle Carnosine Differs</h2>\n<p>Muscle carnosine varies widely between people. Here is what research says about fibre type, training history, sex, age and diet.</p>\n<p>26 October 2026</p>\n<h2>How to Apply Topical Carnosine Gel: A Practical Guide to Timing, Placement and Testing</h2>\n<p>Technique shapes how fairly you can judge any topical product. Here is a practical framework for skin prep, placement, timing and testing.</p>\n<p>19 October 2026</p>\n<h2>Carnosine and Women&#x27;s Sport Performance: What the Research Shows</h2>\n<p>Women appear to carry lower muscle carnosine on average. Here is what that may mean for high-intensity female sport, and where the evidence is still thin.</p>\n<p>12 October 2026</p>\n<h2>Carnosine and Heat Stress: Does Muscle Buffering Matter in Hot Weather?</h2>\n<p>Heat changes how muscle fatigues. Here is what the research says about carnosine, buffering and hot-weather performance, and where the evidence is still thin.</p>\n<p>5 October 2026</p>\n<h2>Carnosine and Post-Exercise Muscle Soreness: Separating DOMS Myths From the Science</h2>\n<p>Lactic acid doesn&#x27;t cause DOMS — muscle microtrauma and inflammation do. Here&#x27;s what the research says about soreness, and where carnosine&#x27;s buffering role fits in.</p>\n<p>28 September 2026</p>\n<h2>Carnosine and Muscle Cramping: What the Science Actually Says</h2>\n<p>The old dehydration theory of cramping is falling apart. Here is what the neuromuscular fatigue model reveals, and why carnosine&#x27;s buffering role is a plausible piece of the puzzle.</p>\n<p>21 September 2026</p>\n<h2>What Tactical Athlete Research Shows About Carnosine and Repeated-Effort Performance</h2>\n<p>Military and tactical training stacks heat, fatigue, and repeated maximal effort in ways few sports do — here&#x27;s what the research on beta-alanine and carnosine in these populations actually shows.</p>\n<p>14 September 2026</p>\n<h2>Why Thoroughbred Horses Are Helping Scientists Understand Human Performance</h2>\n<p>Horses have 6–10× more muscle carnosine than humans. Biopsy studies in thoroughbreds have produced some of the most striking data in the carnosine field. Here&#x27;s what the equine research tells us.</p>\n<p>28 July 2026</p>\n<h2>Carnosine and Recovery: The Role Most People Overlook</h2>\n<p>Most attention on carnosine is focused on performance — buffering, sprint output, delay of fatigue. But carnosine&#x27;s role in recovery may be equally important, and it&#x27;s significantly underappreciated.</p>\n<p>21 July 2026</p>\n<h2>Carnosine vs Beta-Alanine: What&#x27;s the Difference and Which One Actually Works?</h2>\n<p>Beta-alanine has been the go-to for carnosine loading for two decades. But if you understand the mechanism, you see the problem — and why topical carnosine is a fundamentally different approach.</p>\n<p>14 July 2026</p>\n<h2>How Elite Athletes Are Using Carnosine — And Why Most People Haven&#x27;t Heard of It</h2>\n<p>NHL, NFL, NBA, NCAA Division I, European football, Olympic rugby. Over 16,500 athletes have used LactiGo. Here&#x27;s what that adoption actually looks like in practice.</p>\n<p>7 July 2026</p>\n<h2>Lactic Acid Is Not Your Problem. Hydrogen Ions Are. Here&#x27;s What Carnosine Does About It.</h2>\n<p>For decades, athletes blamed lactic acid for the burn and fatigue during intense exercise. The science has moved on. Here&#x27;s what actually causes muscular fatigue — and why carnosine sits right at the centre of it.</p>\n<p>30 June 2026</p>\n<h2>Why Oral Carnosine Doesn&#x27;t Work — And What Topical Delivery Changes</h2>\n<p>Oral carnosine supplements are almost entirely destroyed in the gut before reaching muscle tissue. Here&#x27;s the science of why that happens, and what transdermal delivery actually looks like.</p>\n<p>23 June 2026</p>\n<h2>The Rugby Sevens Study: What Happened When Elite Athletes Used Topical Carnosine</h2>\n<p>In 2025, a peer-reviewed study tested topical carnosine gel on world-class rugby sevens players. The results are some of the most compelling data in applied sports performance research.</p>\n<p>16 June 2026</p>\n<h2>What Is Carnosine? The Science Behind Muscle Performance</h2>\n<p>Carnosine is one of the most concentrated compounds in human muscle tissue — yet most people have never heard of it. Here&#x27;s what it does, why it matters, and why scientists keep coming back to it.</p>\n<p>9 June 2026</p>"
